 ## Overview
-Voxel Ray Marching with Cube-Level DDA is a GPU-based fragment shader technique for rendering voxel volumes by casting rays through a hierarchical grid of voxel cubes and individual voxels, using a two-level Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) to skip empty space efficiently [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Adaptive Ray Marching with Macrocell DDA](1500-adaptive-ray-marching-with-macrocell-dda.md) by replacing the adaptive macrocell traversal with a fixed two-level DDA structure: a coarse traversal over $16 \times 16 \times 16$ voxel blocks to skip empty regions, followed by a fine traversal over individual voxels within occupied blocks to find the first opaque surface [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm is implemented as a reusable fragment shader that supports opaque, transparent, and shadow passes, with the opaque pass stopping at the first opaque voxel along the ray [S1, unofficial].
+Voxel Ray Marching with Cube-Level DDA is a GPU-based fragment shader technique for rendering voxel volumes by casting rays through a hierarchical grid of voxel cubes and individual voxels, using a two-level Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) to skip empty space efficiently [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Adaptive Ray Marching with Macrocell DDA](1500-adaptive-ray-marching-with-macrocell-dda.md) by replacing the adaptive macrocell traversal with a fixed two-level DDA structure: a coarse traversal over $16 \times 16 \times 16$ voxel blocks to skip empty regions, followed by a fine traversal over individual voxels within occupied blocks to find the first opaque surface [S1, unofficial].
 
